---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="ALL-LLLM Pod — Ghost Audio">
  <main class="ghost-page">
    <a class="back" href="/projects/all-lllm-pod/">← ALL-LLLM Pod</a>

    <header class="hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">TRANSCRIPT RECONSTRUCTION</p>
      <h1>Ghost Audio</h1>
      <p class="lede"><em>An investigation into transcript text omissions</em></p>

      <p>
        <strong>Ghost Audio</strong> is a transcript failure mode where speech is clearly
        present in the source audio but absent from the transcript.
      </p>

      <p>
        It is easy to miss because the transcript can still look complete. Missing speech
        quietly changes dialogue, context, and the training data built from it.
      </p>

      <p>
        While auditing Episode 136 of the All-In Podcast, I found two Ghost Audio events
        seconds apart. One had survived in an older transcript but disappeared from later,
        otherwise superior pipelines. The other existed only in the source audio.
      </p>

      <p>
        That discovery led to a month-long investigation into the acoustic stack, decoder
        behavior, and a workflow for surfacing similar omissions elsewhere in the corpus.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section>
      <h2>Why omissions matter</h2>

      <p>
        A missing utterance can preserve grammatical continuity while destroying meaning.
      </p>

      <details class="accordion-block">
        <summary><span>Why Omissions Matter: Featuring <em>The Empire Strikes Back (1980)</em></span></summary>

        <div class="video-block">
          <video controls>
            <source src="/all-lllm-pod/ghost-audio/sw_ghost.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
          </video>
          <div class="video-caption">SOURCE VIDEO · THE EMPIRE STRIKES BACK (1980)</div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout">
          <p>
            This staged example removes Vader's central reveal:
            <code>"No. I am your father."</code>
          </p>

          <p>
            Nothing appears broken in the transcript. Luke still reacts emotionally,
            but the cause of that reaction is gone. A downstream model would see Luke
            rejecting his own accusation, not responding to new information from Vader.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="comparison-grid">
          <div class="comparison-card">
            <h3>Movie Screenplay</h3>
            <div class="transcript-box">
              <p><strong>Vader:</strong> "If only you knew the power of the dark side. Obi-Wan never told you what happened to your father."</p>
              <p><strong>Luke:</strong> "He told me enough. He told me you killed him!"</p>
              <p class="ghost-line"><strong>Vader:</strong> "No. I am your father."</p>
              <p><strong>Luke:</strong> "No. No. That's not true! That's impossible!"</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="comparison-card">
            <h3>Ghost Audio Transcript</h3>
            <div class="transcript-box">
              <p><strong>Vader:</strong> "If only you knew the power of the dark side. Obi-Wan never told you what happened to your father."</p>
              <p><strong>Luke:</strong> "He told me enough. He told me you killed him!"</p>
              <p class="ghost-vanish-line"><strong>Vader:</strong> "No. I am your father."</p>
              <p><strong>Luke:</strong> "No. No. That's not true! That's impossible!"</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="accordion-block">
        <summary><span>Why Omissions Matter: Featuring <em>The Dark Knight (2008)</em></span></summary>

        <div class="video-block">
          <video controls>
            <source src="/all-lllm-pod/ghost-audio/out.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
          </video>
          <div class="video-caption">SOURCE VIDEO · THE DARK KNIGHT (2008)</div>
        </div>

        <div class="callout">
          <p>
            YouTube ASR failed to transcribe Batman's single-word response:
            <code>"Them?"</code>
          </p>

          <p>
            The word marks the moment Batman realizes there are two hostages rather than one.
            Nothing else in the transcript makes the omission obvious.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="comparison-grid">
          <div class="comparison-card">
            <h3>Movie Screenplay</h3>
            <div class="transcript-box">
              <p><strong>Joker:</strong> "There's only minutes left, so you'll have to play my little game if you want to save one of them."</p>
              <p class="ghost-line"><strong>Batman:</strong> "Them?"</p>
              <p><strong>Joker:</strong> "You know, for a while there, I thought you really were Dent."</p>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="comparison-card">
            <h3>Ghost Audio Transcript</h3>
            <div class="transcript-box">
              <p><strong>Joker:</strong> "There's only minutes left, so you'll have to play my little game if you want to save one of them."</p>
              <p><strong>Joker:</strong> "You know, for a while there, I thought you really were Dent."</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </details>
    </section>

<section>
      <h2>Discovery — Episode 136</h2>

      <p>
        In early 2026, I moved to Libsyn as the canonical source for All-In Podcast episodes
        and reprocessed several older episodes as a quality check.
      </p>

      <p>
        Episode 136 exposed the problem.
      </p>

      <p>
        Around t≈21s, Friedberg says <strong>"Oh, my god."</strong> The line was clearly
        audible and had survived in an early transcript, but was absent from every later
        pipeline.
      </p>

      <p>
        Seconds later, guest Brad Gerstner says the same phrase. His line was missing from
        every transcript. I only found it because I happened to be watching the source video
        while investigating Friedberg's omission.
      </p>

      <div class="comparison">
        <div>
          <h3>Ground truth</h3>
          <p><strong>Chamath:</strong> "...shortest that's the fattest, and the fattest that's the dumbest."</p>
          <p class="recovered"><strong>Friedberg:</strong> "Oh, my god."</p>
          <p><strong>Friedberg:</strong> "He's not even here. You can't do that. It's awful."</p>
          <p class="recovered"><strong>Gerstner:</strong> "Oh, my god."</p>
          <p><strong>Friedberg:</strong> "That's just bullying. That's awful."</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>Ghost Audio transcript</h3>
          <p><strong>Chamath:</strong> "...shortest that's the fattest, and the fattest that's the dumbest."</p>
          <p><strong>Friedberg:</strong> "He's not even here. You can't do that. It's awful."</p>
          <p><strong>Friedberg:</strong> "That's just bullying. That's awful."</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section>
      <details class="accordion-block">
        <summary><span>Ruling Out Audio Failure</span></summary>
        

      <p>
        Before blaming the decoder, I worked through the acoustic stack to establish whether
        the missing speech actually existed in the signal.
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li>VAD onset and offset thresholds were varied</li>
        <li>RMS energy confirmed voiced activity in both ghost regions</li>
        <li>pYIN detected traces of Friedberg's pitch after spectral subtraction</li>
        <li>cross-attention showed weak but non-zero activity in both ghost regions</li>
        <li>speaker embeddings placed Friedberg's ghost inside Chamath's local embedding space</li>
      </ul>

      <div class="figure">
        <a href="https://pdf-sorcerer.github.io/alp/residual.png" target="_blank">
          <img
            src="https://pdf-sorcerer.github.io/alp/residual.png"
            alt="Libsyn spectral subtraction with pYIN F0"
          />
        </a>
        <p>
          Libsyn spectral subtraction — pYIN F0 on raw versus residual audio,
          t=19–30s.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="figure">
        <a href="https://pdf-sorcerer.github.io/alp/attention_both_ghosts.png" target="_blank">
          <img
            src="https://pdf-sorcerer.github.io/alp/attention_both_ghosts.png"
            alt="Whisper cross-attention around both Ghost Audio regions"
          />
        </a>
        <p>
          Whisper cross-attention filtered to audio-aligned heads. Attention is visible
          around Friedberg's ghost region and weaker, but present, around Gerstner's.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p>
        The audio existed. The model was seeing it.
      </p>

      <p>
        The question became: why wasn't it decoding it?
      </p>
    
      </details>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Decoder Fork</h2>

      <p>
        At timestamp anchor <code>&lt;|18.98|&gt;</code>, hundreds of possible decoder
        paths opened up, but two mattered.
      </p>

      <div class="decoder-grid">
        <article>
          <p class="eyebrow">GHOST PATH · GROUND TRUTH</p>
          <h3>"Oh, my god."</h3>

          <table>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Oh</td><td>89.24%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>,</td><td>49.93%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>my</td><td>97.67%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>god</td><td>90.67%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>.</td><td>90.67%</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <p><strong>Joint: 35.78%</strong></p>
        </article>

        <article>
          <p class="eyebrow">WINNER PATH · PRODUCTION OUTPUT</p>
          <h3>"He's not even here."</h3>

          <table>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>He</td><td>45.29%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>'s</td><td>99.94%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>not</td><td>99.96%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>even</td><td>99.98%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>here</td><td>99.78%</td></tr>
              <tr><td>.</td><td>92.01%</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>

          <p><strong>Joint: 41.53%</strong></p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <p>
        The decoder skipped Friedberg's earlier utterance and jumped ahead to his next spoken
        words. The ground-truth path lost by 5.75 percentage points.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Timestamp-Token Finding</h2>

      <p>
        The practical result was simpler than the custom decoder work: the production
        WhisperX pipeline could recover Friedberg's ghost by enabling timestamp tokens.
      </p>

      <div class="comparison">
        <div>
          <h3>Timestamp Tokens OFF</h3>

          <p>
            <strong>18.873–19.914</strong><br />
            And the fattest, that's the dumbest.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>25.317–26.097</strong><br />
            He's not even here.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <h3>Timestamp Tokens ON</h3>

          <p>
            <strong>18.873–19.914</strong><br />
            And the fattest, that's the dumbest.
          </p>

          <p class="recovered">
            <strong>20.834–21.495</strong><br />
            Oh, my god.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>25.317–26.097</strong><br />
            He's not even here.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>
        That reduced the problem from a custom research pipeline to something detectable
        inside the production stack.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>BERD-1</h2>

      <p>
        BERD-1 operationalizes the finding by comparing two production-equivalent
        WhisperX Beam=5 transcripts:
      </p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Timestamp Tokens OFF</strong> — <code>without_timestamps=True</code></li>
        <li><strong>Timestamp Tokens ON</strong> — <code>without_timestamps=False</code></li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        When the timestamp-enabled transcript contains speech absent from the
        timestamp-disabled transcript, BERD-1 generates a candidate repair for human review.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section>
      <details class="accordion-block">
        <summary><span>Experimental Decoder Behavior</span></summary>


      <p>
        A separate PyTorch Whisper pipeline at Beam=1 produced localized repetition and
        hallucination in the same difficult regions.
      </p>

      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Start</th>
            <th>End</th>
            <th>Text</th>
            <th>Flag</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>18.863</td><td>19.897</td><td>And the fattest, that's the dumbest.</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td>20.850</td><td>21.499</td><td>Oh, my god.</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td>22.046</td><td>23.100</td><td>Oh, my god.</td><td>⚠ localized repetition</td></tr>
          <tr><td>23.444</td><td>25.020</td><td>I'm bullying when you're bullying.</td><td>⚠ localized hallucination</td></tr>
          <tr><td>25.386</td><td>26.063</td><td>He's not even here.</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td>26.125</td><td>26.638</td><td>You can't do that.</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td>26.803</td><td>27.032</td><td>It's awful.</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td>27.364</td><td>28.902</td><td>I'm bullying when you're bullying.</td><td>⚠ localized hallucination</td></tr>
          <tr><td>29.024</td><td>29.702</td><td>That's just bullying.</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td>29.723</td><td>30.077</td><td>It's awful.</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><td>30.243</td><td>30.942</td><td>I'm bullying when you're bullying.</td><td>⚠ localized hallucination</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    
      </details>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>What I Found</h2>

      <ul>
        <li>Friedberg's ghost was successfully recovered</li>
        <li>BERD-1 surfaced roughly <strong>5–8 additional candidates</strong>, depending on settings</li>
        <li>most were short disfluencies such as <code>uh</code>, <code>ah</code>, and <code>right</code></li>
        <li>the custom Whisper pipeline produced localized hallucinations around otherwise difficult-to-transcribe speech</li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The corpus-wide impact remains unknown and is probably small.
      </p>

      <p class="closing">
        But Ghost Audio is no longer invisible. It can be surfaced, reviewed, and repaired.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
